package com.veilbreaker.companion

/** Additional field worksheets supplement the cited guide; examples are not measurements. */
internal object GuideReferenceDetails {
    fun sections(id: String): List<Pair<String, String>> {
        val worksheet = when (id) {
            "expanded-guide-wifi" -> "BEFORE: Mark each floor, test-client model, required applications and agreed pass criteria. Record AP inventory and permitted access.\nAT EACH POINT: Save the point ID, position source, client orientation, BSSID/band/channel, RSSI and sample age. Separate passive scan observations from associated-link and service tests.\nAFTER: Repeat questionable points and boundaries at representative load. Report untested areas and avoid extrapolating a smooth coverage surface from sparse points. A passive survey does not directly measure client throughput. Background: Cisco WLAN site-survey guidance, https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/support/docs/wireless/5500-series-wireless-controllers/116057-site-survey-guidelines-wlan-00.html"
            "expanded-guide-fwa" -> "BASELINE: Record modem model, firmware, SIM, current antenna/cable and service-test target. Log band, technology, cell identifier and the receipt age of each exposed power and quality reading.\nTRIAL: Alternate candidate positions rather than testing each only once. Record antenna orientation, height, mounting surface and weather; allow the modem to settle and note cell or band changes. Compare both directions and latency at the same target.\nDECISION: Preserve contradictory trials and missing fields. A crowd-sourced site is a candidate, not proof of a serving tower; handset tests do not characterize an installed outdoor antenna system."
            "expanded-guide-das" -> "PREPARE: Agree on carriers, representative devices, pass criteria and accessible floors; mark test locations and construction on the current plan. Record whether each point is GPS, manually placed or unplaced.\nCOLLECT: Keep carrier, SIM, LTE/NR technology, band, cell and sample age with power, quality and repeatable service tests. Record room, corridor, door state and operator-observed obstacles separately from sensor output.\nREPORT: Retest failures and neighboring locations; include untested rooms and absent metrics. A shared cell ID or a strong signal does not identify a physical DAS remote."
            "expanded-guide-rf" -> "PATH WORKSHEET: Record coordinates and elevation datum, antenna heights above their actual mounting surfaces, path length, center frequency, channel bandwidth, polarization, cable type and length. Identify the date and resolution of terrain data.\nBUDGET: For each direction, add transmitter power and directional antenna gains; subtract feedline, component and modeled path losses. Compare the result with sensitivity for the selected mode and compute remaining margin.\nVALIDATE: Note terrain gaps, vegetation and weather assumptions. Inspect Fresnel clearance and measure the installed link in both directions; the free-space result is not a coverage prediction."
            "expanded-guide-network" -> "ISOLATION WORKSHEET: Record interface, IP version, address/prefix, gateway, resolver, VPN/proxy state, target and timestamp. Compare local gateway, remote IP, name resolution and intended application in that order when appropriate.\nCONTROL: Use the same client, destination, direction and interval for comparisons. A DNS success does not prove TCP reachability; ICMP filtering can make a reachable service look unavailable.\nHANDOFF: Identify the first observed failing dependency and retain both successful and failed tests. TCP throughput depends on path RTT, window, bottleneck and test conditions; see RFC 6349, https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6349.html"
            "expanded-guide-cell" -> "IDENTITY FIRST: Record selected subscription, data interface, technology, carrier, band, serving role, identity fields, device and sample age. Keep each LTE and NR observation separate.\nCHECK POWER AND QUALITY: Repeat power and quality readings at fixed locations, then compare service tests against a consistent endpoint and direction. A favorable RSRP alone does not prove good SINR, throughput or reliable uplink.\nINTERPRET: If a sample is old, missing, ambiguous or the modem has switched cell or band, label it before comparison. A PCI can be reused and cannot establish a physical tower location."
            "expanded-guide-fiber" -> "WORKSHEET: Identify fiber type, wavelength, length, connector pairs, splices, splitters and optics transmit/receive ranges. Record the reference method, test cord and instrument calibration before taking readings.\nCOMPARE: Build a loss budget for the actual path and compare it with measured insertion loss, including the uncertainty and reference method. Also verify the receiver is neither below sensitivity nor above its overload limit.\nRETEST: Inspect, clean and reinspect using site safety procedures, then isolate sections if necessary. Follow equipment-specific laser safety; never look into fiber. Background: FOA loss guidance, https://www.thefoa.org/tech/loss-est.htm"
            "expanded-guide-poe" -> "POWER WORKSHEET: Record switch/injector model, per-port standard and limit, available shared budget, cable path, length and device startup/peak load. Identify whether each number is configured, rated, negotiated or measured.\nISOLATE: Compare events with device restarts and simultaneous load. A known-good rated source and cable can separate source, path and device faults; use approved equipment and change procedures.\nVERIFY: Confirm stable operation at representative peak load and verify the data link independently. A port allocation does not necessarily equal the device's actual consumption."
            "expanded-guide-comparison" -> "COMPARABILITY: Pair points by known location and floor, test purpose, instrument, receiver settings, subscription, technology, cell/band and time window. Record actual changes between visits and preserve original source IDs.\nEXAMPLE: If throughput increases after a change but the server or interface also changed, report the values as observations rather than attributing the increase to the intervention. Repeat on the original path.\nPRESENT: Include before/after values, sample count, missing/partial points and contradictory results. Do not average incompatible sources or treat a manually placed point as GPS."
            "expanded-guide-spectrum" -> "BASELINE: Record receiver and antenna, range, sample rate, gain, bin width, sweep duration, time and environment; retain the original capture. Note whether power is calibrated.\nCOMPARE: Keep settings and equipment fixed when possible and repeat at different times and positions. Record a feature's frequency, approximate width and persistence as observations, without naming the source by shape alone.\nFOLLOW UP: Compare timing with independent network and service evidence; a spectral feature alone cannot establish interference, channel utilization or the transmitter. Use suitable calibrated or demodulating equipment when identification is needed."
            else -> return emptyList()
        }
        return listOf("Field worksheet" to worksheet)
    }
}
